WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:03.120
DALE MARKOWITZ: Some
people are born athletes,

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.190
other people are born Mathletes.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:05.730
And me?

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.960
Well, I've been failing
gym since the second grade.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.585
So I always thought
it would make sense

00:00:09.585 --> 00:00:12.480
to take something simpler,
like software engineering.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.850
And this basically describes
my lifelong relationship

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.560
with sports until
a couple of months

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.050
ago when I learned that my
friend, Zack Akil, who's

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.030
both a software
engineer, but also

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.610
a competitive rugby player,
was working on a project

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.872
to analyze soccer kicks
using machine learning.

00:00:25.872 --> 00:00:28.080
So in this episode of "Making
With Machine Learning,"

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.870
we'll talk to Zack
to see how he did it.

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.727
And then I'll try to adapt his
techniques to my tennis serve.

00:00:32.727 --> 00:00:34.560
Stick around to see how
I did it and how you

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.140
can do it with your own data.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.100
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.480
So I wanted to talk
to you because I

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.270
heard that you built a
really cool demo at London

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:49.430
Next this year, using
machine learning

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:53.300
to analyze how people
were able to kick a soccer

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.670
ball for a penalty shot?

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:56.545
Did I get that right?

00:00:56.545 --> 00:00:57.212
ZACK AKIL: Yeah.

00:00:57.212 --> 00:00:59.080
Everything except
for the soccer bit.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:00.730
It was actually a football demo.

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:01.190
DALE MARKOWITZ: Football?

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:03.108
It didn't look that way
to me from the video.

00:01:03.108 --> 00:01:04.400
ZACK AKIL: No, it was football.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.830
We call it football over here.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:08.240
DALE MARKOWITZ: Yeah.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.700
So Zack, why did you
decide to build this demo?

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.370
ZACK AKIL: Well, we have a
partnership with the Football

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:17.060
Association, and we were just
sort of spitballing ideas

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:20.420
as to what would be a really
cool way of highlighting both

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.762
the sport and some
really cool things you

00:01:22.762 --> 00:01:23.970
can do with machine learning.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.820
DALE MARKOWITZ: So what exactly
were you doing with that demo?

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:27.210
I wish I could have been there.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:27.877
ZACK AKIL: Yeah.

00:01:27.877 --> 00:01:30.860
So we were using three
different cameras

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:35.480
to track people's accuracy,
power, and the very style

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:36.500
of their kick.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.855
They go on to the pitch,
they line up the ball.

00:01:38.855 --> 00:01:41.057
We give them the
instructions-- three kicks,

00:01:41.057 --> 00:01:42.140
and they all get recorded.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:43.970
I was there with a whistle.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.345
The people who
were organizing it

00:01:45.345 --> 00:01:47.137
didn't know I was going
to bring a whistle,

00:01:47.137 --> 00:01:48.170
but I brought it anyway.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:48.680
I'd blow the whistle.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:49.722
They would kick the ball.

00:01:49.722 --> 00:01:52.100
They would try to hit one of
two targets in the top left

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.570
and top right corners.

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:56.720
After three kicks, they
would see on a live dashboard

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.650
the results of the power,
the accuracy, and the style

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:02.330
of each one of their kicks.

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:06.380
After that, they would get
their own player profile card.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:07.880
DALE MARKOWITZ: So
tell me about how

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.639
you took all of this
machine learning technology,

00:02:10.639 --> 00:02:14.770
and you put it into
an actual phone app.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:15.680
ZACK AKIL: Ooh.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:19.850
So that's a good
question because we

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:23.510
did have three mobile
phones recording

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:24.830
from different angles.

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:29.010
We have one camera placed
down on the ground.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:32.180
Its only job is to track
the ball on its way

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:34.910
to the goal from the side.

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:36.920
And by tracking the
ball from this angle,

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:39.050
we can easily see the
speed of the ball.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:43.310
And then the next camera we have
is positioned above head height

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:46.490
and behind the kicker,
looking directly at the goal.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:50.070
And this camera tracks where
the targets are on the goal.

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:53.630
And then the last
camera, that's positioned

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:56.360
kind of at a 45 degree
angle, facing the kicker.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:00.140
And this is purely for
the Video Intelligence API

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:02.150
to run its pose estimation on.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:05.150
The API will pick
out the coolest shot,

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:07.970
and then that frame is used
on the player profile card.

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:09.650
For tracking the
ball's speed, we

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.360
did some high
school mathematics,

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.870
where it was the difference
in space over time

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:16.880
to work out the
speed of the ball.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.520
And then to work out
accuracy, because we also

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.830
had binding boxes
for the targets

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:24.080
within the goal
returned, we just

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:27.070
did like a Euclidean distance
to the nearest target.

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:29.730
So that was accuracy
and speed pretty easy.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.560
And then the last one was style.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:33.830
I mean, how can you
measure style, really?

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:36.560
So we had to just come up
with our own metric of what

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:38.135
was the most stylish kick.

00:03:38.135 --> 00:03:39.860
DALE MARKOWITZ: So
what went into it?

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.615
What was the technology
stack that powered your demo?

00:03:42.615 --> 00:03:44.990
ZACK AKIL: For the kind of
really cool stuff, the machine

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:48.410
learning, we used
AutoML to build

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:52.970
the tracking models of the ball
and the targets and the goal.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:56.280
And to analyze the
posture of the player,

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.160
we were actually
using a feature that's

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:00.063
soon to be released,
called Pose Estimation.

00:04:00.063 --> 00:04:02.480
DALE MARKOWITZ: The feature
that Zack's talking about here

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:04.040
is actually called
Person Detection.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.660
And it's now available through
the Video Intelligence API.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:07.820
ZACK AKIL: When
you give it a video

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:09.278
and there's people
in the video, it

00:04:09.278 --> 00:04:12.080
will estimate the
skeleton, and where it is,

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:13.350
and where each joint is.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:14.690
DALE MARKOWITZ: What are all of
the different machine learning

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:16.847
problems that you faced
as you were building this?

00:04:16.847 --> 00:04:19.430
ZACK AKIL: The first challenge
that we knew we wanted to solve

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:20.750
was just tracking the ball.

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:24.860
We figured, OK, a football
is a pretty generic object.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:27.450
Maybe there's a pre-trained
model that we can utilize.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:31.610
And what we found was when
you take a picture or a frame

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.860
from a video of a kick,
the ball is too blurry

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:36.950
to then be picked up by a
generic pre-trained model.

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:38.700
And that was the first
challenge, was OK,

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:41.510
we had to train our
own vision model

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:43.310
to detect a blurry football.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:45.950
So I built two auto ML models.

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:49.560
Here's the first one, which is
the tracking of the accuracy.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:52.250
And you can see here,
if I click on image,

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:54.380
you can see where we've
labeled the images.

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.415
Using the tool, we draw
boxes around things.

00:04:56.415 --> 00:04:58.040
So if I want to draw
a box around that,

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:01.275
we drew a box around it and told
it what we wanted it to track.

00:05:01.275 --> 00:05:02.900
DALE MARKOWITZ: So
you have two targets

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:06.205
that are on the goalpost and the
ball annotated in this model?

00:05:06.205 --> 00:05:06.830
ZACK AKIL: Yep.

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:08.330
Exactly, yeah.

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:10.598
After we've labeled some
data, to train a model

00:05:10.598 --> 00:05:11.390
it was really easy.

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:13.520
You just go to the Train
tab, and there's a button.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.370
And you can see here we actually
trained a couple of models.

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:18.370
By clicking Train Your
Model, it will give us

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:20.120
some questions about
it, like, how long do

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:20.930
we want to train the model for?

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:23.030
But it was effectively
just that one click,

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:25.530
and we would get a new model.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:28.880
So here's an example of
the predictions it makes

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:30.600
and how it ranks itself.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:33.170
So we've got this camera
looking from the side view

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:35.450
to track the ball going across.

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:36.940
And you can see
in this instance,

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:37.940
the ball's a bit blurry.

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:40.050
And that's why we needed
to train a custom model,

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:43.320
because a generic
pre-trained model didn't

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.210
recognize this as a football.

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:48.270
DALE MARKOWITZ: So do you
play soccer, slash, football?

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:50.220
ZACK AKIL: No, I hate football.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:51.288
I'm more into rugby.

00:05:51.288 --> 00:05:53.580
DALE MARKOWITZ: Now that I
understood Zack's technique,

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:54.955
I went to the
tennis court to see

00:05:54.955 --> 00:05:57.090
if I could use it to
improve my tennis serve.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.650
I used the Video
Intelligence API

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:00.720
to track my skeleton,
which let me calculate

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:02.910
the angles of my arms and legs.

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:04.410
And I trained an
AutoML Vision model

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:05.952
to track the trajectory
of the tennis

00:06:05.952 --> 00:06:09.292
ball and calculated speed
as it zoomed into the net.

00:06:09.292 --> 00:06:11.250
The math revealed what
I'd always sort of known

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:12.510
about my tennis serve--

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:13.372
it stinks.

00:06:13.372 --> 00:06:15.330
But check out my blog
linked in the description

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:18.190
to see how I'm using
these stats to get better.

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:19.240
That's all for now.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:20.310
See you next time.

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:22.460
[MUSIC PLAYING]

